What the program actually does: [38;5;10mcontainer_of(&t.a, struct test, a)[0m and [38;5;10mcontainer_of(&t.b, struct test, b)[0m both recover [38;5;10m&t[0m, so the output is just [38;5;10mt.a + t.b[0m. I modelled the struct as an 8-byte buffer with a [38;5;10m(offset)[0m pointer stand-in so the macro's offset subtraction and member reads are reproduced literally in safe Rust rather than collapsed to [38;5;10ma + b[0m.[0m[0m
